paragraph:
7-40. Graph your data for “The Penny Lab” carefully on the sheet protector of the Lesson 7.1.5 Resource Page.
a. How can you describe the scatter plot representing all teams’ Penny Lab data? Consider:
• where the graph crosses the y-axis;
• whether the graph is linear or curved;
• whether there are any asymptotes;
• whether the graph is continuous or discrete;
• the end behavior;
• whether the graph is increasing or decreasing; and
• the percent rate of change.
b. Write an equation for a function that models the data. Make sure you also write a “let” statement for your variables.
c. What output does your function give for x = 0? What could this mean in relation to the situation?
d. Could there be an output value for x = −1? If so, what might it mean?
e. What could be the domain of the model that you wrote in part (b)? With this domain, what would a graph of your model look like?
f. What family of functions that you saw in previous chapters has graphs like the one you made in part (e)? What are the first few values of this function? What is the equation?
